From: postmarketOS rhodep port
Subject: [PATCH] rpmsg: glink: take a reference for the rcid entry too

qcom_glink_native_remove() releases one reference per channel for the lcids
idr and one more for the rcids idr:

	idr_for_each_entry(&glink->lcids, channel, cid)
		kref_put(&channel->refcount, qcom_glink_channel_release);

	idr_for_each_entry(&glink->rcids, channel, cid)
		kref_put(&channel->refcount, qcom_glink_channel_release);

but only one of those two insertions ever takes a reference.
qcom_glink_send_open_req() does a kref_get() before adding the channel to
lcids. qcom_glink_rx_open() adds it to rcids and takes a reference only when
it allocated the channel itself, which its own error path spells out:

	free_channel:
		/* Release the reference, iff we took it */
		if (create_device)
			kref_put(&channel->refcount, qcom_glink_channel_release);

For a channel the remote opened that is fine: the reference from
qcom_glink_alloc_channel() covers the rcids entry, and the later
qcom_glink_send_open_req() adds the one for lcids. Two references, two puts.

For a channel opened from this side it is not. A local open allocates the
channel, qcom_glink_send_open_req() takes the second reference for lcids, and
when the remote answers with its own OPEN, qcom_glink_rx_open() finds the
channel in lcids and inserts it into rcids taking nothing. Two references, and
qcom_glink_native_remove() then puts three times.

Locally opened channels are what userspace gets through rpmsg_ctrl's
RPMSG_CREATE_EPT_IOCTL, so this is reachable from an unprivileged-looking
sequence: open an endpoint on a glink channel the remote also opens, then let
the remote be torn down. On a modem crash that path runs as

	rproc_crash_handler_work -> rproc_trigger_recovery -> rproc_boot_recovery
	-> rproc_stop -> glink_subdev_stop -> qcom_glink_smem_unregister
	-> qcom_glink_native_remove

and it lands exactly as advertised:

	refcount_t: underflow; use-after-free.
	WARNING: lib/refcount.c:28 at refcount_warn_saturate+0xd8/0x13c,
	         CPU#6: kworker/u32:5/128
	Workqueue: rproc_recovery_wq rproc_crash_handler_work
	Call trace:
	 refcount_warn_saturate+0xd8/0x13c (P)
	 qcom_glink_native_remove+0x160/0x268
	 qcom_glink_smem_unregister+0x28/0x934 [qcom_glink_smem]
	 glink_subdev_stop+0x1c/0x30 [qcom_common]
	 rproc_stop+0xb4/0x144
	 rproc_boot_recovery+0x30/0xc4
	 rproc_trigger_recovery+0x70/0x84
	 rproc_crash_handler_work+0xa8/0xe0

Observed on a Motorola Moto G82 5G (SM6375) with an AT console open on the
modem's DS glink channel when the modem crashed.

Take the reference when the rcids entry is created rather than only when the
channel is allocated, and release it unconditionally on the error path, which
is correct for both cases: in the create_device case the allocation reference
is the one being dropped, and otherwise it is the one just taken.

--- a/drivers/rpmsg/qcom_glink_native.c
+++ b/drivers/rpmsg/qcom_glink_native.c
@@ -1652,6 +1652,13 @@ static int qcom_glink_rx_open(struct qcom_glink *glink, unsigned int rcid,
 
 		/* The opening dance was initiated by the remote */
 		create_device = true;
+	} else {
+		/*
+		 * The channel is already in lcids and holds a reference for
+		 * that entry. qcom_glink_native_remove() puts one reference
+		 * per idr, so the rcids entry needs one of its own.
+		 */
+		kref_get(&channel->refcount);
 	}
 
 	trace_qcom_glink_cmd_open_rx(glink->label, name, channel->lcid, rcid);
@@ -1706,9 +1713,8 @@ rcid_remove:
 	channel->rcid = 0;
 	spin_unlock_irqrestore(&glink->idr_lock, flags);
 free_channel:
-	/* Release the reference, iff we took it */
-	if (create_device)
-		kref_put(&channel->refcount, qcom_glink_channel_release);
+	/* Release the rcids reference, whichever of the two it was */
+	kref_put(&channel->refcount, qcom_glink_channel_release);
 
 	return ret;
 }
